// Encyclopedic info text for lineage nodes, shown in the card info popover.
// Keyed by the exact tree-node name. Sourced from the breed-info document.
// REWRITTEN IN PLAIN ENGLISH, 25 September 2026 (owner: the originals were written
// for adults, with words like landrace, stock and fixed). Every fact is kept; the
// words are simpler, specialist terms are explained where they stay, and each
// entry names its dog. breedInfoLong below is unchanged.
export const breedInfo: Record<string, string> = {
  "Affenpinscher": "The Affenpinscher is a tiny, scruffy German dog with a funny, monkey-like face. Its name means monkey terrier. It was kept in kitchens and stables to catch rats and mice, and it comes from the same family as the Pinschers and Schnauzers.",
  "Afghan Hound": "The Afghan Hound is a long-haired hunting dog from the mountains of Afghanistan. It hunted by sight, chasing animals at speed over cold, rocky ground. In our family tree, its ancestors are the ancient sighthounds of the East. Today it is a glamorous show dog and pet.",
  "Alpine Mastiff farm dogs": "Alpine mastiff farm dogs were big, heavy dogs that guarded farms high in the mountains. They gave later mountain and farm dogs their size, strong bones and thick, weatherproof coats.",
  "Ancient Arctic Spitz": "The ancient Arctic spitz dogs were close cousins of the wolf, living across the far north of Europe and Asia. They came before any real breeds, and they are the ancestors of the thick-coated spitz dogs, like huskies, that came later.",
  "Ancient Chinese toy dogs": "Ancient Chinese toy dogs were little pet dogs kept in palaces and homes in China. Their faces were not as flat as today's Pugs and Pekingese, but they were already being bred to be small and cuddly companions.",
  "Ancient Molossers": "The Ancient Molossers were big, powerful guard and war dogs from ancient Greece, Rome and the Middle East. They had broad heads and strong bodies, but they were not as heavy as today's mastiffs.",
  "Ancient Spitz dogs": "The ancient spitz dogs were the first dogs with the spitz look: pointy ears that stand up, thick fluffy coats, wedge-shaped heads and tails that curl up over their backs.",
  "Ancient Techichi dogs": "The Techichi was a small, sturdy pet dog kept in ancient Mexico, long before Europeans arrived. It was a bit chunkier than today's Chihuahua, which comes from it.",
  "Ancient eastern sighthounds": "Ancient eastern sighthounds were lean, long-legged dogs from the deserts and grasslands of the East. They hunted by sight, spotting prey from far away and chasing it down. They are the ancestors of the Saluki and other speedy sighthounds.",
  "Arctic sled dogs": "Arctic sled dogs were the spitz dogs of the far north that pulled sleds. They were bred to cope with the cold, to keep going for hours, to work as a team, and to move easily over snow and ice.",
  "Asian flat-faced toy dogs": "Asian flat-faced toy dogs were little pet dogs from the East. They gave later Pugs and toy spaniels their short noses, round heads and gentle, royal-pet manners.",
  "Barbet and water spaniels": "Barbets and water spaniels are Europe's family of shaggy, curly-coated water dogs. They were used to fetch birds that hunters had shot out of marshes, rivers and lakes.",
  "Barbet water dogs": "Barbets were shaggy, curly-coated water dogs from the French marshes, used to fetch birds from the water. They were practical working dogs, never clipped or groomed for shows.",
  "Basset Artesien Normand": "The Basset Artesien Normand is a French hunting dog with short legs and a long body. It is lighter than the modern Basset Hound, and its legs are not quite so short.",
  "Basset and heavy Hounds": "Basset and heavy hounds were short-legged, deep-chested hunting dogs that followed a smell along the ground. They were slow and steady, with strong bones and short legs.",
  "Beagle": "The Beagle was bred as a small pack dog for hunting hares and rabbits. It swapped size for stamina and a brilliant nose. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Talbot and the Southern Hound, with a little Greyhound. It is still a popular breed, and it helped make some later terriers and hunting dogs.",
  "Bichon Frise": "The Bichon Frise is a fluffy white pet dog that looks like a powder puff. It comes from the little white lapdogs of the Mediterranean.",
  "Black and Tan Terrier": "The Black and Tan Terrier, also called the Old English Black and Tan Terrier, was a tough little rat-catcher that is now extinct. Almost every British terrier comes from it. In 1576 a writer called Caius described small terriers creeping underground after foxes and badgers.",
  "Grand Bleu de Gascogne": "The Grand Bleu de Gascogne is a big French hunting dog from Gascony, with a blue speckled coat, a deep, booming bark and a nose that can follow very old trails. It was one of three breeds mixed to make the Dumfriesshire hunting pack, along with the English Foxhound and the Bloodhound.",
  "Border Terrier": "The Border Terrier was bred to keep up with the hunt across rough country, yet still be small enough to follow a fox down its hole. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Bedlington, the Dandie Dinmont and the old fell terriers. It later helped make the Patterdale Terrier, and it is a popular working dog and pet today.",
  "Boston Terrier": "The Boston Terrier was bred in America in the late 1800s from bulldog-and-terrier crosses, made smaller to be a pet. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Bulldog and the old English White Terrier. It is still a popular pet, nicknamed the American Gentleman because of its smart black-and-white coat.",
  "Boxer": "The Boxer was bred in Germany in the late 1800s from the Bullenbeisser, meaning bull-biter, an old German dog that caught boar and bulls, mixed with the Bulldog. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old German boarhounds and the Bulldog. It is a popular working dog and family pet today.",
  "British Pointers": "British Pointers are fast, smooth-coated gundogs that freeze and point their nose at hidden birds to show the hunter where they are. They were made from setting spaniels, hounds, foxhounds and pointers from Europe.",
  "The First Setters": "British Setters are elegant, feathery-coated gundogs. They find birds by smell and then freeze or crouch low to show the hunter where the birds are hiding. They were bred from old setting spaniels.",
  "Buckhound": "The Buckhound was a big British hunting dog, kept in packs to hunt fallow deer. In our family tree, its ancestors include old smell-hunting hounds and the Talbot. The Buckhound died out, but it helped make the later Staghound.",
  "Bull Terrier": "The Bull Terrier comes from bulldog-and-terrier crosses, mixing a bulldog's strength with a terrier's speed and determination. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Bulldog, the English White Terrier and the Dalmatian. It later helped make the Fox Terrier and relatives of the Jack Russell.",
  "Carriage guard dogs": "Carriage guard dogs were bigger stable dogs, a bit like Pinschers, that ran alongside horse-drawn coaches and barked to warn of trouble on the road.",
  "Cavalier King Charles Spaniel": "The Cavalier King Charles Spaniel was bred on purpose to bring back the longer-nosed little spaniels seen in old British paintings. In our family tree, its ancestors include the King Charles Spaniel and older sporting toy spaniels. It is a very popular pet, and it is one parent of the Cavachon and the Cavapoo.",
  "Celtic Hound": "The Celtic Hound was not one single breed, but the hunting dogs kept by the Celtic peoples long ago. In our family tree, it mixes the Celts' own dogs with speedy sighthounds from the East. It matters a lot, because many later British and Irish hounds, chasing dogs and terriers come from it.",
  "Chihuahua": "The Chihuahua comes from the little pet dogs of ancient Mexico, most likely the Techichi. In our family tree, it comes from the Techichi, with some small European pet dogs mixed in later. It is the smallest dog breed in the world.",
  "Chukchi sled dogs": "The Chukchi sled dogs were bred by the Chukchi people of north-east Siberia to pull light sleds quickly over long distances, and to be good companions. The Siberian Husky comes straight from them.",
  "Collie": "The Collie comes from Britain's old working sheepdogs. It kept their cleverness and their shape, and became a breed that people recognise today.",
  "Collie or working dog": "The collie or working dog was the general British farm dog, used for herding. It sat somewhere between the rough-and-ready farm dogs of old and the collies we know today.",
  "Continental toy Spaniels": "The continental toy spaniels were small spaniels kept at the royal courts of Europe. Some had floppy ears and some had early butterfly ears, and they are the ancestors of the Papillon and the Phalene.",
  "Old Cumberland herding dogs": "The old Cumberland herding dogs were sheepdogs from the north of England, similar to the old collies. They probably helped make the Border Collie through their work on the northern hills.",
  "Dalmatian": "The Dalmatian was a carriage and working dog, built on older spotted hunting dogs, with some pointer and coach-dog blood mixed in.",
  "Deerhound": "The Deerhound is a rough-coated Scottish sighthound, bred to chase deer across the rugged Highlands.",
  "Doberman Pinscher": "The Doberman was created in Germany in the late 1800s by a tax collector who wanted a dog to protect him. It mixes a Pinscher's sharpness with a guard dog's strength. In our family tree, its ancestors include the German Pinscher, Rottweiler-like butchers' dogs and old black-and-tan dogs. It is a working dog and a pet today.",
  "Earth and hunt terriers": "Earth and hunt terriers were tough British working terriers. Some went underground after foxes and badgers, and some ran with the hunt, before today's terrier breeds existed.",
  "Eastern Lion dogs": "Eastern lion dogs were little Asian pet dogs, bred to look like lions, which were seen as special or sacred. They are the ancestors of the Pekingese, the Tibetan breeds and similar toy dogs.",
  "English Water Spaniel": "The English Water Spaniel was a British water dog with a curly or wavy coat, used to fetch birds from water. It was half spaniel, half water dog, and it is now extinct.",
  "English White Terrier": "The English White Terrier was made for Victorian dog shows, bred from old English rat-catching terriers into a slim, white dog. In our family tree, its ancestors are the old white and black-and-tan terriers. The breed disappeared within a few decades, but it lives on in the Bull Terrier, the Fox Terrier and relatives of the Jack Russell.",
  "Farm and kitchen curs": "Farm and kitchen curs were ordinary household dogs, often short or scruffy. They caught rats, turned the roasting spits in kitchens, guarded homes and did all sorts of farm jobs.",
  "Fishermen's water dogs": "Fishermen's water dogs worked for people on the coast and on rivers. They fetched things from the water, pulled nets, guarded boats and helped the fishermen.",
  "French Bulldog": "The French Bulldog began with small bulldogs that lace workers from Nottingham took with them to France. It was bred into a city pet with bat-like ears. In our family tree, it comes from the Bulldog, with some small pet dogs mixed in. It is one of the most popular pets today.",
  "German Pinscher": "The German Pinscher is a sleek, smooth-coated German dog that caught rats in stables and guarded the yard. It is the smooth-haired cousin of the scruffier Schnauzer farm dogs.",
  "German Shepherd Dog": "The German Shepherd Dog was created from the local sheepdogs of Germany, especially those from Thuringia and Wurttemberg. It became a proper breed for herding, police and service work.",
  "German bracke scenthounds": "The German brackes were hunting dogs that followed a trail by smell. They are cousins of Britain's early hounds, from the same old European family, but they grew into their own branch in mainland Europe.",
  "German farm Spitz": "German farm spitz dogs were alert, fluffy-coated guard dogs on farms in central Europe. Rather than pulling sleds like Arctic spitz dogs, they watched over the farm, the house and the chickens.",
  "Great Dane": "The Great Dane grew from the old German boarhounds, which mixed a mastiff's strength with a sighthound's long legs for speed when hunting dangerous wild boar. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old German boarhounds and the Ancient Molossers. Today it is a gentle giant, much calmer than its hunting past.",
  "Heavier working Spaniels": "Heavier working spaniels were broad, low, solid hunting spaniels. They helped make the Sussex Spaniel, the Clumber Spaniel and other heavier gundogs.",
  "Highland mainland terriers": "Highland mainland terriers were scruffy, compact Scottish working dogs that went underground after foxes and badgers. They are the ancestors of the Cairn Terrier, the Scottish Terrier and the Westie.",
  "Irish Setter": "The Irish Setter was bred from older Irish setting dogs into a fast, stylish red gundog that finds game birds. In our family tree, its ancestors include the English Setter, the Irish Water Spaniel, the Gordon Setter and the Pointer. It later helped make the Golden Retriever, and it is still its own breed today.",
  "Italian Greyhound": "The Italian Greyhound is a tiny, elegant sighthound from the Mediterranean, like a Greyhound shrunk down to be a pet.",
  "Land Spaniels": "Land spaniels were early hunting spaniels that worked on land, chasing birds out of hiding for hawks, nets and later guns. Springers, cockers and setters all split off from them later.",
  "Local German cattle dogs": "Local German cattle dogs were farm dogs that drove and guarded cattle and sheep, before today's cattle dogs and sheepdogs became breeds.",
  "Local red ratting Terriers": "Local red ratting terriers were small, lively, scruffy red terriers that caught rats in stables and on farms in East Anglia. They are the ancestors of the Norwich Terrier.",
  "Low-slung soldiers' dogs": "Low-slung soldiers' dogs were small, tough dogs kept in army camps. Soldiers valued them for being hardy, easy to carry and useful, not for being a pure breed.",
  "Maltese": "The Maltese is a tiny white lapdog with a silky coat, from an ancient Mediterranean family. It was a favourite of rich and noble families, and it is related to the Bichon.",
  "Mastiff": "The Mastiff is today's version of Britain's very old, heavy guard dogs. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Ancient Molossers and the old British bandogs. The same family also led to the Bulldog, the Bull Terrier and other bulldog-and-terrier dogs.",
  "Mastiff and Alaunt war dogs": "Mastiff and Alaunt war dogs were powerful medieval dogs used for grabbing and holding animals, for guarding and for battle. They were sporty and tough, not heavy like today's show mastiffs.",
  "Mediterranean Bichon lapdogs": "Mediterranean bichon lapdogs were small white or cream lapdogs with silky or softly wavy coats. They are the ancestors of the Maltese, the Bolognese and the Bichon Frise.",
  "Mediterranean miniature sighthounds": "Mediterranean miniature sighthounds were small, elegant chasing dogs from the ancient Mediterranean, used for small game or kept as pets. They are behind the Italian Greyhound and dogs like the Whippet.",
  "Miniature Schnauzer": "The Miniature Schnauzer was bred down in size from the Standard Schnauzer farm dog, but kept its sharp rat-catching skills. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old Schnauzer farm dogs and the Affenpinscher. It is the most popular Schnauzer today.",
  "Mountain coursing Hounds": "Mountain coursing hounds were long-legged sighthounds from the mountains of Central Asia, built for sharp eyes, speed and rough, high ground. They gave the Afghan Hound its thick coat.",
  "Native Irish Terriers": "Native Irish terriers were scruffy, hard-working Irish farm dogs that caught rats and other pests. They came in all sorts of colours and coats before today's Irish terrier breeds existed.",
  "Newfoundland landrace dogs": "Newfoundland landrace dogs were the big, strong local working dogs of Newfoundland. Landrace means a local kind of dog shaped by its work and home, rather than a planned breed. They pulled loads, worked in the water and guarded homes, and they are behind the modern Newfoundland and some retrievers.",
  "Norfolk Spaniel": "The Norfolk Spaniel was a group of broad English hunting spaniels, rather than a proper breed. In our family tree, its ancestors include land spaniels and water spaniels. In the end it was mixed into the English Springer Spaniel.",
  "Norwich terrier stock": "The Norwich terriers came from small red and black-and-tan rat-catching dogs in East Anglia, including stable dogs and the terriers kept by Cambridge students. They later became the Norwich and Norfolk Terriers.",
  "Old Border Terriers": "The old Border terriers were scruffy hill terriers from the border between England and Scotland. They were narrow, weatherproof and tough enough to go after foxes with the hunt.",
  "Old British Bandogs": "Old British bandogs were guard dogs kept chained up in yards. They were broad and powerful, but more practical and less heavy than today's mastiffs.",
  "Old British ratting Terriers": "Old British ratting terriers were small, sharp dogs, smooth or scruffy, that caught rats and mice in barns, towns and stables, before today's terrier breeds existed.",
  "Old English Bulldog": "The Old English Bulldog was much sportier than today's Bulldog. It was bred to grab and hold on, and it was used in bull-baiting, a cruel old sport, rather than kept as a pet. In our family tree, its ancestors include mastiffs, Alaunt war dogs and old British bandogs. The Bulldog, the Bull Terrier and the Staffordshire Bull Terrier all come from it.",
  "Old English White Terrier": "The Old English White Terrier was a delicate white terrier bred for dog shows from English working terriers. It is now extinct, but it helped make other breeds.",
  "Old European lapdogs": "Old European lapdogs were small pet dogs kept by rich and royal families in Europe from the Renaissance onwards. They included dogs like toy spaniels, bichons and early toy poodles.",
  "Old European water dogs": "Old European water dogs were shaggy, curly or woolly dogs that worked in water. They are behind the Barbet, the Poodle and the water dogs of Portugal, Spain and Britain.",
  "Old German boarhounds": "The old German boarhounds were big, sporty dogs, half mastiff and half hound, used to hunt dangerous wild boar. The Great Dane comes from them, more than from the heavy mastiffs.",
  "Old German farm guards": "The old German farm guards were strong, sturdy dogs that guarded farmyards and animals from medieval times onwards. They were all-rounders, before German dogs split into special breeds.",
  "Old German hunting dogs": "The old German hunting dogs were not one breed but a whole group: dogs that hunted by smell, dogs that hunted wild boar and dogs that followed tracks.",
  "Old German Ratters": "The old German ratters were scruffy or smooth dogs that caught rats in stables. They came from the Pinscher and Schnauzer family, before the small ratting breeds existed.",
  "Old Highland Terriers": "The old Highland terriers were tough, stocky, scruffy Scottish dogs in earthy colours that went underground after foxes and badgers. The Westie, the Cairn and the Scottie all split off from them later.",
  "Old Irish water dogs": "The old Irish water dogs were scruffy brown or dark water dogs that fetched birds from the water. They came before the modern Irish Water Spaniel.",
  "Old Scotch Collie": "The Old Scotch Collie was the Scottish shepherd's sheepdog before there were dog shows. It was elegant but hard-working, and the Rough, Smooth and working collies all come from it.",
  "Old Welsh Land Spaniels": "The old Welsh land spaniels were tough red-and-white Welsh dogs that chased birds out of hiding for hunters. They are the ancestors of the Welsh Springer Spaniel, but scruffier.",
  "Old black-and-tan Setters": "The old black-and-tan setters were early setting dogs with dark coats and tan patches. They are behind the Gordon Setter and similar setters.",
  "Old earth Terriers": "Old earth terriers were compact, scruffy dogs bred to go down holes after foxes, badgers and other animals. Many Scottish and British terriers come from them.",
  "Old fell Terriers": "The old fell terriers were tough terriers from the hills of northern England. Their legs were long enough to keep up with the hunt, and their bodies narrow enough to squeeze between rocks after foxes.",
  "Old hill and bearded Collies": "The old hill and bearded collies were shaggy, tough British dogs that herded sheep and drove cattle in the hills. They worked in a looser, more relaxed way than the later Border Collie.",
  "Old hunting dogs of the Celts": "The old hunting dogs of the Celts were early hounds and chasing dogs in western Europe. Owning a good one was a sign of importance, and they varied from place to place.",
  "Old scenting Hounds": "Old scenting hounds were long-eared, deep-voiced European and British dogs that followed a trail with their noses. They are behind the Talbot, the Southern Hound, the brackes and similar dogs.",
  "Old setting spaniels": "Old setting spaniels were early bird-finding dogs that crouched low or froze when they smelled birds, to show the hunter where they were. All setters come from them.",
  "Old short-legged working dogs": "Old short-legged working dogs were a mixed group of low dogs used in kitchens, on farms, down burrows and for catching rats, before the short-legged breeds we know existed.",
  "Old sporting toy Spaniels": "Old sporting toy spaniels were small spaniels that were still sporty. They were little enough to be pets, but kept a proper nose, longer legs and a love of hunting.",
  "Old Toy Spaniels": "The old toy spaniels were early lap spaniels kept at the royal courts of Europe. They had silky, feathery coats and fairly long noses, before the flatter-faced King Charles look came along.",
  "Old wirehaired fell Terriers": "The old wire-haired fell terriers were shaggy, weatherproof terriers from the hills of northern England. They were rougher and more mixed than the later Lakeland and Patterdale Terriers.",
  "Old working bandogs": "Old working bandogs were guard dogs kept chained up around farms, yards and big estates. They were powerful but sporty, and also used to grab and hold animals.",
  "Old working collies": "The old working collies were Britain's everyday herding dogs, the ones all later collie breeds come from. They were rougher, darker and less elegant than the Old Scotch Collie.",
  "Paisley Terrier": "The Paisley Terrier was a silky Scottish terrier, kept for shows and as a pet, and closely related to the Skye Terrier. In our family tree, its ancestors include Skye terriers and old Highland terriers. The breed died out, but it lives on in the Yorkshire Terrier and several other small terriers.",
  "Papillon": "The Papillon is the butterfly-eared cousin of the little toy spaniels that appear in centuries of European royal paintings. Papillon is French for butterfly. In our family tree, it comes from those royal toy spaniels. It is one of the cleverest toy breeds today.",
  "Parisian Ratters": "Parisian ratters were small, scruffy dogs that caught rats in the city of Paris. They were lighter and more mixed than British terriers or the German Schnauzer ratters.",
  "Continental water dogs": "Continental water dogs were the curly-coated water dogs of mainland Europe, from neatly clipped water retrievers to shaggy, bearded French marsh dogs. Old records are not clear enough to say which of them helped make the Irish Water Spaniel.",
  "Pomeranian": "The Pomeranian is the smallest of the German farm spitz dogs. It was bred smaller and smaller through the 1800s, helped along by Queen Victoria, who loved them. In our family tree, it comes from the German farm spitz and the ancient spitz family. It is a toy pet today.",
  "Pug": "The Pug is a small, flat-faced dog with a smooth coat and a curly tail. It comes from the flat-faced pet dogs of Asia.",
  "Pug-type toy dogs": "Pug-type toy dogs were earlier smooth-coated pet dogs from Asia. They had short noses, but not as short as today's Pug.",
  "Roman drover dogs": "Roman drover dogs were working dogs that moved and guarded cattle for the Romans, travelling with their herds, camps and armies along their roads. They helped shape later European working dogs.",
  "Rottweiler": "The Rottweiler is a German cattle, butcher's and guard dog. It comes from the cattle-driving and farm guard dogs of the town of Rottweil.",
  "Rough northern sighthounds": "Rough northern sighthounds were chasing dogs from cold countries, with longer coats. They had the build of desert sighthounds, but were made for mountains and cold northern land.",
  "Rough water dogs": "Rough water dogs were shaggy, practical dogs that worked in water, with coats that protected them from the cold. They came before spaniels, poodles and retrievers split into separate breeds.",
  "Saint Bernard": "The Saint Bernard comes from the mountain farm dogs kept by the monks at the Great St Bernard Hospice in the Alps. They were bred to be big and weatherproof, with famous noses for finding lost travellers. In our family tree, its ancestors include Alpine mastiffs and the Ancient Molossers. It is a gentle giant pet today.",
  "Schnauzer-type farm dogs": "Schnauzer-type farm dogs were medium-sized, scruffy, bearded dogs from southern Germany that caught rats in stables. The Schnauzers and Pinschers all come from them.",
  "Setter": "The Setter is the later gundog version of the old setting spaniel. It is feathery-coated and elegant, and bred to find birds and freeze to show the hunter where they are.",
  "Shaggy upland herders": "Shaggy upland herders were rough-coated sheepdogs that worked on hills and mountains. Their weatherproof coats kept them going in wild, windy places.",
  "Shih Tzu": "The Shih Tzu was bred at the Chinese royal court from older Tibetan and Chinese palace dogs, purely to be a pet. Its name means lion dog. In our family tree, its ancestors include the ancient Chinese toy dogs and Asian flat-faced toy dogs. It is a popular pet today.",
  "Siberian Husky": "The Siberian Husky comes from the Chukchi sled dogs of north-east Siberia, bred to pull sleds for long distances and to live closely with their families. In our family tree, it comes from the Chukchi dogs and the Arctic sled dogs. It is a sled dog and pet today.",
  "Skye Terrier stock": "The Skye terriers were long, low, scruffy dogs from the Isle of Skye that went underground after foxes and badgers. They are behind the Skye Terrier, the Paisley Terrier and other Scottish terriers.",
  "Small imported dogs": "Small imported dogs were little pet dogs brought into Britain and Europe from abroad. They often helped shape royal lapdogs, toy spaniels and small city dogs.",
  "Southern Hound": "The Southern Hound was a big, slow English hunting dog with a deep, booming bark, built to follow a trail carefully by smell. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Talbot and the St Hubert Hound. It matters hugely, because the Beagle, the Foxhound, setters, some terriers and many other British hounds come from it.",
  "Spitz-type dogs": "Spitz dogs are a big family of northern dogs with pointy ears that stand up, curly tails and thick double coats. The Arctic sled dogs and Europe's farm spitz dogs both come from this family.",
  "St Hubert Hound": "The St Hubert Hound was a tracking dog bred by monks at the abbey of St Hubert in Belgium. It is the ancestor, or a very close cousin, of the Bloodhound.",
  "St Hubert and Talbot hounds": "The St Hubert and Talbot hounds were heavy medieval hunting dogs with long ears and amazing noses, which followed a trail slowly and steadily.",
  "St John's Water Dog": "The St John's Water Dog was a smaller fishing dog from Newfoundland. It had a smooth coat, usually black with white patches, and it is the most important ancestor of all the retrievers.",
  "Schnauzer farm dogs": "The Standard Schnauzer farm dogs were the medium-sized version of the old scruffy German dogs that caught rats and guarded the yard, and the type we recognise as a Schnauzer today.",
  "Talbot hound": "The Talbot Hound was a famous white medieval hunting dog, heavy, with long ears and a brilliant nose. It is now extinct, but it is one of the most important ancestors of Britain's hounds.",
  "Talbot hounds": "Talbot hounds were old white hunting dogs that followed a trail by smell. They are behind the Southern Hound, the Bloodhound and the early British hunting packs.",
  "Teckel (Dachshund) family": "The Teckel, or Dachshund, family are Germany's short-legged hunting dogs, made especially for going after badgers and foxes underground.",
  "Thuringian herding dogs": "Thuringian herding dogs were smaller, sharper, fox-like sheepdogs from northern Germany. They gave the German Shepherd much of its alert, quick character and its pointed, upright ears.",
  "Tibetan temple dogs": "Tibetan temple dogs were small but sturdy dogs that kept watch over monasteries in the Himalayas. They had long coats and feathery tails, and their faces were less flat than the Chinese palace dogs.",
  "Tweed Water Spaniel": "The Tweed Water Spaniel was a water dog from the Scottish Borders, used to fetch birds from rivers and rough country. In our family tree, its ancestors include water spaniels and the St John's Water Dog. It died out, but it lives on in the Golden Retriever and the Goldendoodle.",
  "Water spaniel and Collie": "Water spaniel and Collie means a mix of water dogs and sheepdogs, which helped shape some later retrievers and farm dogs.",
  "Water Spaniels": "Water spaniels were curly or wavy-coated spaniels that fetched birds from marshes, rivers and lakes, before today's water spaniels and retrievers became breeds.",
  "Wavy-Coated Retriever": "The Wavy-Coated Retriever was Britain's early retriever. It mixed the St John's Water Dog with setters, spaniels and possibly collies, before it was bred into the Flat-Coated Retriever.",
  "Weimaraner": "The Weimaraner was bred at the German court of Weimar as an all-round hunting dog, first for big animals and later for birds. In our family tree, its ancestors include the German brackes and European pointers. It is a gundog and a pet today.",
  "Welsh Corgi": "The Welsh Corgi is a short-legged Welsh farm dog that herded cattle by nipping at their heels. It is related to the spitz dogs, not the terriers or hounds.",
  "Welsh herding dogs": "Welsh herding dogs were long-legged, practical dogs that drove cattle and herded sheep. They were more varied and worked in a looser way than today's Border Collies.",
  "West Highland White Terrier": "The Westie was bred from the white puppies born among Scotland's old Highland terriers. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Cairn, the Scottish and the Skye Terrier. It is still a popular breed, and it helped make the Sealyham and Lucas Terriers.",
  "Whippet": "The Whippet was bred as a smaller sighthound for racing and catching rabbits. It was fast enough for races but easy for working people to keep. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Greyhound, the black-and-tan terrier and the Italian Greyhound. It later helped make Longdogs, some terriers and toy terriers, and it is a popular breed today.",
  "White English Terrier": "The White English Terrier was a white show terrier bred from English rat-catching terriers. It is now extinct, but it matters because the Bull Terrier and its relatives come from it.",
  "Wire Fox Terrier": "The Wire Fox Terrier is a wiry-coated terrier for shows and work, which comes from the old scruffy hunt terriers that went down holes after foxes.",
  "Working hunt Terriers": "Working hunt terriers were practical terriers that ran with the hunt and chased foxes out of their holes. They were narrow, tough and useful, before terriers were bred for the show ring.",
  "Wurttemberg Sheepdogs": "Wurttemberg sheepdogs were bigger, heavier, calmer sheepdogs from southern Germany. They gave the German Shepherd its size, strong bones and steady nature.",
  "Ancient Mastiff": "The Ancient Mastiff was a powerful early British dog used for guarding, hunting and grabbing large animals, valued for its strength rather than its looks. In our family tree, its deeper roots are the Ancient Molossers and the Alaunt war dogs. Its exact family history is lost, but dogs like it led to the Mastiff, the bandogs and later the Bulldog.",
  "Celtic Coursing Hound": "The Celtic Coursing Hound was built for speed across open ground, hunting by sight for the Celts. We think it came from the speedy hounds of Gaul and older sighthounds from the East, rather than being one breed. It lives on most clearly in the Greyhound, the Deerhound and the Wolfhound.",
  "Celtic Heeler": "The Celtic Heeler was a short, tough cattle dog that herded by nipping at the cows' heels, keeping low to dodge their kicks. Its family history is pieced together from old Welsh herding dogs and short-legged working dogs. We think dogs like it helped make the Welsh cattle dogs that became the Corgis.",
  "Celtic Scent Hound": "The Celtic Scent Hound was the Celts' nose-led hunting dog, built to follow a trail by smell rather than chase what it could see. We think it came from the Segusian hounds and the scent-hunting dogs of the ancient Mediterranean. The rache, the Talbot, the Southern Hound and many later British hounds come from it.",
  "Irish Wolfhound": "The early Irish Wolfhound was a huge dog used to chase and guard against wolves and other big animals. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Celtic hounds and the heavy Ancient Molossers. The breed is still with us, but the ancient working dogs were probably more varied than today's giant show dogs.",
  "Livestock Dog": "The Livestock Dog stands for the working dogs that guarded, moved and looked after farm animals before Britain had named breeds. We think the Celts' herding dogs and the Romans' sheepdogs both helped make them. From them came drovers' dogs, curs, heelers and, in time, the local collies and cattle dogs.",
  "Bearded Collie": "The Bearded Collie is a shaggy Scottish sheepdog, built to herd animals in wild weather and tough country. In our family tree, it sits between the old working collies and the shaggy lowland sheepdogs. Its family also helps explain how the Old English Sheepdog came about.",
  "English Foxhound": "The English Foxhound became the great pack hound of big country estates, bred for stamina, a loud bark and chasing together over long distances. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Southern Hound, the Talbot and the Greyhound. It went on to help make many setters, terriers, hounds and retrievers.",
  "Otterhound": "The Otterhound was bred for a hard job: following the smell of otters across land and through cold water. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Bloodhound, the Southern Hound and shaggy water dogs. There are very few left today, but it also helped make several terriers.",
  "Skye Terrier": "The Skye Terrier comes from the scruffy Highland terriers that went underground after foxes and badgers. Its long body and thick coat suited the harsh weather of the islands. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old Highland terriers and Skye terriers. It later helped make several small British terriers, including the Westie and the Yorkshire Terrier.",
  "Staghound": "The Staghound was a big pack hound bred for the tough job of hunting red deer. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Southern Hound, the English Foxhound and the Buckhound. It died out as hunting changed.",
  "Turnspit Dog": "The Turnspit Dog had one of the strangest jobs in British history. It ran inside a wheel, like a hamster, to turn meat roasting over the fire. We think it came from short-legged working dogs and ordinary farm and kitchen dogs. When machines took over the job, the dog disappeared.",
  "Clumber Spaniel": "The Clumber Spaniel was bred as a heavy, steady spaniel that pushed slowly through bushes to chase out birds, rather than racing ahead. In our family tree, its ancestors include land spaniels and heavier hounds. It is still with us, and it is behind the later Cocker and Field Spaniels.",
  "Cur": "The Cur was a drover's dog, valued for moving cattle along the roads for long days rather than for its looks. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old working collies and short-legged heelers. It disappeared when cattle stopped being walked to market, but it lives on in later cattle dogs and heelers.",
  "Dandie Dinmont Terrier": "The Dandie Dinmont is a Borders terrier bred for tough work underground and by the water, catching pests, even though it has a funny, fluffy topknot. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old Border terriers, Skye terriers and a little Otterhound. It later helped make the Bedlington, the Border and the Lakeland Terriers.",
  "English Setter": "The English Setter was bred to find game birds and then freeze or crouch low to show the hunter where they were, the same job as the old setting spaniels. In our family tree, its ancestors include old setters, the Pointer and water spaniels. It later helped make the Irish Setter and the retrievers.",
  "Greyhound": "The Greyhound is Britain's version of a very old kind of dog that hunts by sight, chasing at top speed across open ground. In our family tree, it comes from the Celtic hounds, with older sighthounds from the East. An amazing number of later hounds, terriers, lurchers and racing dogs have Greyhound in them.",
  "King Charles Spaniel": "The King Charles Spaniel is a small royal spaniel, bred mainly as a pet, but with the shape and family of older sporting toy spaniels. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old European toy spaniels and flat-faced dogs from Asia. It later helped make the Cavalier and several modern crosses.",
  "North Country Beagle": "The North Country Beagle was a faster hare-hunting dog from the north of England, lighter and quicker than the old Southern Hound. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Southern Hound and the Talbot. It faded away as modern Beagle packs became more alike.",
  "Old English Sheepdog": "The Old English Sheepdog began as a practical dog for driving cattle and sheep, not as a fluffy show dog. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Bearded Collie and Welsh herding dogs. The breed is still with us, even though its old droving job has mostly gone.",
  "Old Welsh Grey Sheepdog": "The Old Welsh Grey Sheepdog was a shaggy Welsh hill dog, built for herding in tough mountain country. In our family tree, its ancestors include Welsh herding dogs and older shaggy hill dogs. It disappeared when more standard collie breeds took over its job.",
  "Pointer": "The Pointer was bred to find game birds and then stand completely still, pointing, so the hunter knew exactly where they were. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Foxhound, the Greyhound, the Bloodhound and setters, because early gundogs were made from several kinds of working dog. It went on to help make many setters, terriers and retrievers.",
  "Scottish Terrier": "The Scottish Terrier grew out of the tough Highland terriers, built low and strong for catching pests among rocks and rough ground. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old Highland terriers and Skye terriers. It is still its own breed, and it helped shape the West Highland White Terrier.",
  "Cumberland Sheepdog": "The Cumberland Sheepdog was a northern working collie used for farm and hill work. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old working collies and Welsh herding dogs. It stopped being a separate kind of dog when it was mixed into the Border Collie.",
  "Dumfriesshire Hound": "The Dumfriesshire Hound was a tall Scottish hound, bred for tough fox hunting in the hills of Dumfries and Galloway. In our family tree, its ancestors include the English Foxhound, the Bloodhound and the French hounds of Gascony. It did not survive as a modern breed.",
  "Lucas Terrier": "The Lucas Terrier was made as a smaller, easier working terrier, with a calmer nature than some tougher terriers. In our family tree, it comes from the Sealyham and Norfolk Terriers. It is still with us, but there are very few of them.",
  "Norfolk Terrier": "The Norfolk Terrier is the floppy-eared version of the little East Anglian rat-catching terriers, which were once lumped in with the Norwich. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Norwich terriers, the Irish Terrier and the Yorkshire Terrier. It is still with us, and it helped make the Lucas Terrier.",
  "Northern Inuit Dog": "The Northern Inuit Dog is a modern British pet breed, made to look like a wolf without being one. It was made by mixing Arctic sled dogs with the German Shepherd. It is a new breed, so its story is about its looks and nature rather than an old working job.",
  "Norwich Terrier": "The Norwich Terrier grew from small red rat-catching and stable dogs, and was later bred into a neat terrier with ears that stand up. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Irish Terrier, the Yorkshire Terrier and local red ratting terriers. It is still with us, though there are not very many.",
  "Patterdale Terrier": "The Patterdale is a hard-working fell terrier from northern England, bred to be useful underground rather than to look good at shows. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old fell terriers, the Border Terrier and the Bedlington. It is still a working dog, and Patterdales can look quite different from each other, because being good at the job always came first.",
  "Toy Bulldog": "The Toy Bulldog was a Victorian attempt to make a tiny pet version of the old Bulldog. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Old English Bulldog and small Pug-like dogs. It had disappeared by the early 1900s, and no modern breed clearly carries it on.",
  "Cavachon": "The Cavachon is a modern mix of the Cavalier King Charles Spaniel and the Bichon Frise. Its story is simple: two popular pet breeds mixed together, rather than an old working dog. It is a new cross, so no other dogs come from it.",
  "Cavapoo": "The Cavapoo is a modern mix of the Cavalier King Charles Spaniel and the Poodle. It brings together the Cavalier's toy-spaniel family and the Poodle's water-dog family in one small dog. It is a new cross, so no other dogs come from it.",
  "Cockapoo": "The Cockapoo is a mix of the Cocker Spaniel and the Poodle, and one of the first designer crosses to become well known. Its two parents bring together the land spaniels and Europe's water dogs. It is a modern pet, not the start of a new family of dogs.",
  "Goldendoodle": "The Goldendoodle is a modern mix of the Golden Retriever and the Poodle. Behind those two parents are retrievers, water dogs, setters and spaniels, so its family tree is surprisingly big. It is a new kind of pet and assistance dog.",
  "Jackapoo": "The Jackapoo is a mix of the Jack Russell Terrier and the Poodle, joining a fox-hunting terrier family to an old European water-dog family. That gives it a much bigger and more mixed family tree than its name suggests. It is a new cross.",
  "Labradoodle": "The Labradoodle was first bred from the Labrador and the Poodle to be an assistance dog. Its parents bring together the retrievers from Newfoundland and Europe's old water dogs. It is a modern cross, not an old breed.",
  "Maltipoo": "The Maltipoo is a modern mix of the Maltese and the Poodle. Its family joins an old line of little pet dogs with the even older water dogs behind the Poodle. It is a new cross, so no other dogs come from it.",
  "Bedlington Terrier": "The Bedlington was bred as a fast, tough working terrier, even though today's show dogs look like soft little lambs. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old Border terriers, the Whippet and the Dandie Dinmont. It later helped make the Border, Lakeland and Patterdale Terriers.",
  "Longdog": "A Longdog is not a breed, but a cross between two sighthounds, usually made to be as fast as possible for chasing. In our family tree, its ancestors are mainly the Greyhound, the Whippet and the Deerhound. Because it is a working cross rather than a breed, no later dogs come from it.",
  "Lurcher": "The Lurcher is not one breed, but a working cross, usually a sighthound mixed with a tougher or easier-to-train dog. In our family tree, the Greyhound is joined by collies or other working dogs and some old black-and-tan terrier. It was the poacher's favourite all-round dog, built to be useful rather than pure-bred. Its job is much older than its name: in 1576 John Caius wrote about the Tumbler and the Stealer, small greyhound-like dogs that caught rabbits by trickery and at night. The word lurcher, from an old word meaning to lurk or steal, was first used for a dog in 1668.",
  "Manchester Terrier": "The Manchester Terrier was bred as a city rat-catcher and a star of rat-baiting, a cruel Victorian contest where a dog was put into a walled pit full of rats while people bet on how fast it could catch them. The contests were held in the back rooms and cellars of pubs, especially in London, and were most popular in the 1840s, before the RSPCA helped stamp them out. A Manchester breeder called John Hulme crossed a Black and Tan Terrier with a Whippet to make a dog quick enough for rat pits and rabbit races, and by 1860 the town had given the breed its name. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Black and Tan Terrier and the Whippet. It later helped make the English Toy Terrier and the Lancashire Heeler.",
  "Staffordshire Bull Terrier": "The Staffordshire Bull Terrier comes from the old bulldog-and-terrier crosses, mixing a bulldog's power with a terrier's speed and determination. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Bulldog and old black-and-tan terriers. It lived on after cruel dog fighting was banned, and became a much-loved pet that still has its compact, muscly build.",
  "Welsh Terrier": "The Welsh Terrier was bred as a compact black-and-tan working dog, for chasing foxes and badgers and catching pests on farms. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old English black-and-tan terriers and the fell terriers. It is still with us today.",
  "Basset Hound": "The Basset Hound was bred as a slow, low hunting dog that keeps its nose close to the ground and moves at a pace people on foot can keep up with. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Bloodhound and the Basset Artesien Normand. The breed is still with us, though today's Bassets have shorter legs and droopier skin than many of the old working ones.",
  "Border Collie": "The Border Collie was bred for careful sheep work, using speed, balance and a hard, steady stare to move the sheep rather than force. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Old Scotch Collie, Welsh herding dogs and the Cumberland Sheepdog. It is one of the best examples in Britain of a breed still shaped by its original job.",
  "Cairn Terrier": "The Cairn Terrier comes from the Highland dogs used to chase rats and foxes out of piles of rocks, called cairns, and rough ground. In our family tree, its ancestors include Skye terriers and mainland Highland terriers. It is still a breed today, and it helped make the West Highland White Terrier.",
  "Cardigan Welsh Corgi": "The Cardigan is the older, long-tailed Welsh Corgi, built low for herding cattle, not as a toy dog. In our family tree, its ancestors include early short-legged hunting dogs and Welsh herding dogs. It later helped make the Pembroke Corgi, and it is still a breed of its own.",
  "Glen of Imaal Terrier": "The Glen of Imaal Terrier was built low, heavy and strong for tough farm work and catching pests in Ireland. In our family tree, its ancestors include native Irish terriers and short-legged dogs linked to soldiers and settlers. It is still with us, but it is quite rare.",
  "Golden Retriever": "The Golden Retriever was bred on purpose in the Scottish Highlands as a gundog with a soft mouth, so it could carry birds without damaging them. In our family tree, its main ancestors are the Wavy-Coated Retriever and the Tweed Water Spaniel, with some setter and Bloodhound. It is a parent of the Goldendoodle and one of the best-known family dogs in the world.",
  "Gordon Setter": "The Gordon Setter was bred as a strong black-and-tan bird dog, suited to Scottish country and working at a steadier pace than some lighter setters. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old black-and-tan setters, the Bloodhound and collies. It also helped make the Irish Setter and, through it, the Golden Retriever.",
  "Irish Terrier": "The Irish Terrier grew from Ireland's farm and rat-catching dogs into a fast, bold terrier that could do any job. In our family tree, its ancestors include native Irish terriers and old English black-and-tan terriers. It later helped make the Norfolk and Norwich Terriers.",
  "Labrador Retriever": "The Labrador was bred in Britain from the hard-working water dogs brought over from Newfoundland, and then improved with British hunting dogs. In our family tree, its main ancestor is the St John's Water Dog, with some Pointer, setter and water spaniel. It is one of the most important working dogs and pets today.",
  "Lakeland Terrier": "The Lakeland Terrier was bred for hunting foxes in the Lake District, where a dog needed stamina, courage and a body small enough for rocky fox dens. In our family tree, its ancestors include old fell terriers and wire-haired terriers, the Bedlington and the Wire Fox Terrier. It is still with us, though there are not very many.",
  "Lancashire Heeler": "The Lancashire Heeler kept the old job of herding cattle by nipping at their heels, in a very small body that could dart quickly away from their hooves. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Welsh Corgi and the Manchester Terrier. It is one of the clearest living links to Britain's old cattle-driving dogs.",
  "Pembroke Welsh Corgi": "The Pembroke kept the Welsh cattle dog's job, but grew into a slightly different kind of Corgi from the Cardigan. In our family tree, its ancestors include spitz dogs, the Cardigan and Welsh herding dogs. The breed is still with us, though today most people know it as a pet, and as Queen Elizabeth II's favourite breed, rather than as a cattle dog.",
  "Soft-Coated Wheaten Terrier": "The Wheaten came from Ireland's farm terriers, doing whatever work needed doing around small farms. In our family tree, all its ancestors are native Irish terriers. It is still with us, and it helped make the Kerry Blue Terrier.",
  "Bloodhound": "The Bloodhound is the great tracking expert of the medieval hunting dogs, built to stay on a very old trail by smell rather than chase what it can see. In our family tree, its ancestors include the St Hubert Hound and older smell-hunting hounds. It has helped make many later hounds, terriers, setters and even retrievers.",
  "Drover's Dog": "The Drover's Dog was a working dog used to walk cattle and sheep long distances to market. Nobody kept records of its family, so we think it was a mix of dogs chosen for stamina, control and toughness rather than looks. That working tradition led to the Cur in our family tree.",
  "Earth Dog": "The Earth Dog was not one breed, but the small dogs of medieval times that were sent underground after foxes, badgers and other animals. Nobody kept records of their families, so we think local terriers shared the job and mixed together over hundreds of years. A big part of Britain's later terrier family comes from them.",
  "Rache": "The rache was the medieval pack hound that hunted by smell, while other hounds did different parts of the hunt. In our family tree, its ancestors include the St Hubert Hound, the Talbot and older smell-hunting hounds. The name disappeared as hunting dogs became more specialised.",
  "Scottish Deerhound": "The Scottish Deerhound was built to chase red deer across the open Highlands, with speed plus enough size and strength for such big animals. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Celtic Hound and the rough-coated northern sighthounds. It is still with us, though in small numbers, and it no longer does its old hunting job.",
  "Shepherd's Dogs": "Shepherd's dogs were the working sheepdogs of medieval Britain, before today's collie breeds existed. Nobody kept records of their families, so we think local herding dogs were chosen for control, stamina and teamwork with the shepherd. This working line led to later sheepdogs, including the Old Welsh Grey Sheepdog.",
  "Talbot": "The Talbot was the famous white medieval hound, known for following heavy scent trails and for its place in English hunting. In our family tree, its ancestors include the St Hubert Hound and older smell-hunting hounds, but nobody kept records like we do today. The name died out, but it lives on through the Southern Hound and the Beagle.",
  "Airedale Terrier": "The Airedale was bred as a big, all-round terrier that could catch pests and work in the water around the valley of the River Aire in Yorkshire. In our family tree, its ancestors include old black-and-tan terriers and the Otterhound. It is still with us, and it is the biggest of Britain's traditional terriers.",
  "Bulldog": "Today's Bulldog was bred from the older bull-baiting dog after cruel blood sports were banned, slowly becoming heavier, shorter and more of a pet. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Old English Bulldog, the Mastiff and the Pug. It later helped make the Bull Terrier, the Staffordshire Bull Terrier and their relatives.",
  "Bullmastiff": "The Bullmastiff was created as a gamekeeper's night dog, strong enough to stop a poacher and hold him down without biting him. In our family tree, its ancestors are the English Mastiff and the Old English Bulldog. The breed is still with us, though its old job guarding big estates has mostly gone.",
  "Curly-Coated Retriever": "The Curly-Coated Retriever sits where Britain's water spaniels and water dogs meet, without fitting neatly into either family. In our family tree, its ancestors include the English Water Spaniel, the St John's Water Dog, the Irish Water Spaniel and the Poodle, though the amounts of each are best guesses. It is one of the oldest retriever breeds.",
  "English Toy Terrier": "The English Toy Terrier was bred down in size from the black-and-tan rat-catching terriers, into a smaller pet and show dog. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Manchester Terrier and the Black and Tan Terrier. It is still with us, but there are very few of them.",
  "Flat-Coated Retriever": "The Flat-Coated Retriever was bred as a gamekeeper's all-round retriever, fast, easy to train and a keen swimmer. In our family tree, its ancestors include the St John's Water Dog, setters, water spaniels and sheepdogs. It is still with us, though it never became as popular as the Labrador.",
  "Fox Terrier": "The Fox Terrier came from the hunt terriers used to chase foxes out of their holes after the hounds had chased them underground. In our family tree, its ancestors include old black-and-tan terriers, the English White Terrier, the Beagle and the Bull Terrier. It later became the main parent of the Jack Russell Terrier and, through it, the Jackapoo.",
  "Irish Water Spaniel": "The Irish Water Spaniel was bred as a strong, curly-coated water dog for fetching birds in cold, difficult conditions. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old Irish water dogs, the Poodle and the Barbet, though old records are not clear enough to prove it. It later helped make retrievers and setters.",
  "Jack Russell Terrier": "The Jack Russell was bred for fox hunting, with the size, stamina and courage to run with the hunt and then go down a fox's hole. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Fox Terrier, old white terriers and working hunt terriers. It is a lively terrier today, and one half of the Jackapoo.",
  "Kerry Blue Terrier": "The Kerry Blue grew up as an Irish all-round farm terrier, catching pests, helping with animals and doing other jobs. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Soft-Coated Wheaten and other native Irish terriers. It is still with us, but it is quite rare.",
  "Rough Collie": "The Rough Collie was bred from Scottish working collies into the elegant long-coated dog made famous by dog shows, books and films like Lassie. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old working collies and the hill and bearded collies. Today it is mostly a pet and show dog rather than a farm worker.",
  "Sealyham Terrier": "The Sealyham was built as a compact white working terrier for chasing badgers, foxes and other tough animals. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Dandie Dinmont, the West Highland White and the Wire Fox Terrier. There are only a few today, and it helped make the Lucas Terrier.",
  "Yorkshire Terrier": "The Yorkshire Terrier began among the rat-catching dogs of northern mill towns, before being bred into the tiny, silky pet we know today. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Paisley Terrier, the Skye Terrier and old black-and-tan terriers. It later helped make the Norfolk, Norwich and Lucas Terriers.",
  "Cocker Spaniel": "The Cocker Spaniel was bred as a compact spaniel for chasing woodcock and other birds out of thick bushes. In our family tree, its ancestors include the land spaniels, the Field Spaniel and the Sussex Spaniel. It is a popular breed today, and one half of the Cockapoo.",
  "English Springer Spaniel": "The English Springer Spaniel was bred to find birds and make them spring out of the bushes for the hunter, and then fetch them. In our family tree, its ancestors include the Norfolk Spaniel, water spaniels and the Welsh Springer. It is still one of the most important working gundogs.",
  "Field Spaniel": "The Field Spaniel was bred as a useful land spaniel, before show breeders for a while made it much heavier and longer. In our family tree, its ancestors include land spaniels, the Sussex Spaniel and the Clumber Spaniel. It is a rare working gundog today, and it is behind the Cocker Spaniel.",
  "Sussex Spaniel": "The Sussex Spaniel was bred as a low, steady dog for chasing birds out of thick bushes, where keeping going mattered more than speed. In our family tree, its ancestors include land spaniels and heavier working spaniels. It is a rare breed today, and it helped make the Cocker, Field and Toy Trawler Spaniels.",
  "Toy Trawler Spaniel": "The Toy Trawler Spaniel was a small Victorian pet spaniel made from toy and sporting spaniels. In our family tree, its ancestors include the King Charles Spaniel and the Sussex Spaniel. It died out, and no modern breed comes from it.",
  "Welsh Springer Spaniel": "The Welsh Springer comes from the old red-and-white hunting spaniels of Wales, bred for busy work in rough country. In our family tree, its ancestors include the old Welsh land spaniels and other land spaniels. It is still with us, and it helped make the English Springer.",
};

// Extended write-ups, shown once a circle is clicked into (the short entries in
// breedInfo stay on hover). Added progressively: any name missing from here
// falls back to its short entry in breedInfo, so this can be filled in batches
// without ever leaving a blank box. Keys must match tree-node names in
// data/lineage.ts exactly, including case and apostrophes, or the lookup
// silently falls back to the short text.
export const breedInfoLong: Record<string, string> = {
  // Batch 1 (19 August 2026): collies and herding types. Keys verified against
  // data/lineage.ts by exact name. The first eight had short breedInfo entries
  // already; the last four (Celtic herdsmen's dogs, Roman shepherd dogs,
  // Anglo-Saxon herding dogs, Norse settlers dogs) are live herding-root nodes
  // that had no breedInfo entry at all, so on hover they still show their
  // lineage note and on click they now show these extended write-ups.
  "Old working collies": "Britain's herding landrace, bred on skill with sheep rather than any fixed look, so type varied from farm to farm. They are the root the Rough, Smooth, Bearded and Border collies grew from. There was no studbook: a dog earned its keep by working.",
  "Old Scotch Collie": "The pre-show Scottish shepherd's dog, handsome but still a working animal earning its living on the hill, and the stock behind the Rough, Smooth and Border collies. Queen Victoria admired dogs of this type at Balmoral from the 1860s, which made the farm collie fashionable.",
  "Old hill and bearded Collies": "The shaggy, weatherproof end of the British herding landrace, rough-coated and loose-eyed for driving stock across exposed upland in hard weather. Their coat and droving style carry forward into the Bearded Collie and, through it, help explain the Old English Sheepdog.",
  "Old Cumberland herding dogs": "The northern English branch of the border herding landrace, worked on the fells of Cumberland and the surrounding hill country. Their blood ran into the Cumberland Sheepdog and the Border Collie. It never became a registered breed, fading as the Border Collie took over.",
  "Welsh herding dogs": "A long-legged, loose-eyed droving and sheep-working landrace, racier and more variable than the crouching Border Collie that later dominated, and the parallel Welsh branch of the herding pool. Their blood feeds the Welsh Sheepdog, Ci Defaid Cymreig, still bred as a working landrace today.",
  "Shaggy upland herders": "Rough-coated hill and mountain sheepdogs, their dense weatherproof coats an answer to wind and rain on exposed ground rather than show fashion. That coat carries into the Bearded Collie, the Old Welsh Grey Sheepdog and the Old English Sheepdog, all upland workers.",
  "Collie or working dog": "Collie or working dog is less a breed than a label for the everyday British farm dog: pastoral stock kept for brains and biddability rather than a fixed look. In this reconstruction it stands in where a herding cross fed into another line, carrying collie intelligence without a pedigree. Dogs of this broad type sat behind the drovers, curs and heelers of the working countryside, and only later did the named collie breeds separate from the same pool.",
  "Roman drover dogs": "The Roman drover dogs were Molosser-type cattle dogs brought into Britain with the legions and left behind as Rome's frontier moved north. Broad and powerful, they were bred to move and guard herds on the road rather than to gather sheep with a collie's finesse. Roman farm writers such as Columella and Varro described exactly this kind of working dog. In this reconstruction their blood joins the native herding pool that later produced Britain's drovers and cattle dogs.",
  "Celtic herdsmen's dogs": "The all-round farm dogs of the pre-Roman Celtic tribes, expected to guard, drive and hold livestock rather than specialise, and one of the two oldest roots of Britain's herding tradition. No pedigree survives, so we treat them as a landrace selected for usefulness with stock.",
  "Roman shepherd dogs": "The flock-guarding sheepdogs the Roman farm writers set down in detail, advising that a shepherd's dog be kept white so it could be told from a wolf in the half-light. Brought over with Roman farming, they are the second ancient herding root.",
  "Anglo-Saxon herding dogs": "Collie-sized working dogs of early medieval England, known from excavated bones rather than portraits or pedigrees. Remains of this build have been recovered from Anglo-Saxon settlements at West Stow in Suffolk and at Brandon, a rare physical glimpse of the everyday herding dog.",
  "Norse settlers dogs": "Scandinavian dogs brought to Britain during Viking settlement, most likely spitz-type working animals then mixed into local herding stock. Isotope work on Viking-age remains, including animals cremated with people at Heath Wood in Derbyshire, shows dogs travelling with Norse settlers rather than all being native.",

  // Batch 2 (19 August 2026): terriers and earth dogs, Britain-first. Keys
  // verified against data/lineage.ts by exact name. Most had short breedInfo
  // entries; Ancient Celtic earth dogs and Early badger hunting dogs had no entry
  // (they show a lineage note on hover, these on click). The continental ratters
  // (Old German ratting terriers, Parisian ratters and terriers) were deferred to
  // a later continental batch. The FGF4 short-leg fact in Ancient Celtic earth
  // dogs is text only, never a lineage link, verified against Parker et al.,
  // Science 2009, 325:995-998.
  "Old British ratting Terriers": "The small, sharp vermin dogs kept in barns, stables, town yards and the rat pits, valued only for how fast they killed. A working landrace, not a breed, and one of the deepest terrier roots. Timed rat-killing matches kept the type sharp into Victorian times.",
  "Earth and hunt terriers": "The practical working terriers of the British countryside, split between those small enough to go to ground after fox and badger and those that ran with the hunt. The word terrier comes from the Latin terra, earth: the dog was named for going underground.",
  "Old earth Terriers": "The old earth terriers were the compact, rough-coated dogs bred for one demanding job: to follow fox, badger and otter underground. Narrow-chested and hard, they worked the earths across Britain long before named breeds fixed, and in this reconstruction they are ancestral to many Scottish and British terriers. A true earth dog was judged by span, small enough round the chest for a man's two hands to meet, so it could turn in a tight tunnel.",
  "Working hunt Terriers": "Working hunt terriers were the fox-bolting terriers kept with the hunt, put in when a fox went to ground so the chase continued. Bred hard and narrow for function, not looks, they came before the show terriers refined out of them, and in this reconstruction they feed the Fox Terrier and Jack Russell lines. The Reverend John Russell founded his famous strain from exactly this working stock, buying his first terrier, Trump, from a milkman near Oxford around 1819.",
  "Old fell Terriers": "The hard hill terriers of the northern fells, long enough to follow a foot pack over rough country yet narrow enough to work a fox in rock. Fell packs hunted without horses, so the terrier kept up on foot all day before going to earth.",
  "Old wirehaired fell Terriers": "The shaggier end of the northern hill-terrier stock, their dense broken jackets shedding rain and snow. Rougher and more variable than the later Lakeland, they were kept for fox work in rocky country. The harsh double coat was a working feature, not a show point.",
  "Old Border Terriers": "The rough, narrow working terriers of the Cheviot hills along the English-Scottish border, kept by hill farmers to bolt or kill the fox that raided their lambs. They stand behind the Bedlington, Dandie Dinmont and the Border Terrier, which keeps their otter-shaped head.",
  "Old Highland Terriers": "The rugged earth dogs of the western Highlands and islands, worked among rocks to bolt fox, wildcat and otter, and one stock before the Cairn, Scottie, Westie and Skye. The white Westie line was fixed after Colonel Malcolm shot his own reddish terrier by mistake.",
  "Highland mainland terriers": "The Highland mainland terriers were the rough earth dogs of the Scottish mainland, as against the island strains, worked among rock and heather for fox and vermin. Stocky and weatherproof, they belonged to the same broad Highland working pool, and in this reconstruction they are ancestral to the Cairn, Scottish and West Highland White terriers. The Cairn takes its name from their job: bolting prey from the cairns, the piles of loose stone that made ideal earths.",
  "Skye Terrier stock": "The long, low, heavy-coated earth dogs of Skye and the Western Isles, bred to drive fox, badger and otter from cairn and rock, behind the Skye and Paisley. Its fame owes much to Greyfriars Bobby, said to have guarded his master's grave for fourteen years.",
  "Native Irish Terriers": "The rough, all-purpose farm and vermin terriers of Ireland, varied in colour and coat, kept to kill rats, guard the smallholding and turn a hand to anything. They are the shared stock behind the Irish, Kerry Blue, Wheaten and Glen of Imaal terriers.",
  "Norwich terrier stock": "The Norwich terrier stock came from the small red and black-and-tan ratting dogs of East Anglia, kept in stables and around the region's farms and colleges. Lively and hard for their size, they were a local working type before the Norwich and Norfolk breeds fixed, combining local red ratters with Irish and Yorkshire blood in this reconstruction. They became a craze among Cambridge undergraduates around 1900, when dogs kept in college gave the early strain the nickname 'Trumpington terriers'.",
  "Local red ratting Terriers": "The small, lively red vermin dogs of East Anglia, stable and farm terriers kept for a lifetime's war on rats. A regional working type rather than a fixed breed, they sit behind the Norwich and Norfolk, which are still shown in red, wheaten and grizzle.",
  "Old English White Terrier": "The all-white terrier bred out of the old English ratting stock, prick-eared and slim. Never robust, it died out within decades, though its blood carried into the Bull Terrier, Fox Terrier and Jack Russell. The 1895 ban on ear-cropping hastened its end.",
  "White English Terrier": "The White English Terrier was the show-bench name for the same all-white terrier, a slim, elegant dog bred more for looks than work by the 1860s. In this reconstruction it is one of three names for a single extinct breed. Delicate and prone to the deafness that often follows an all-white coat, it never held on, but it was a key cross behind the Bull Terrier, and appeared at England's earliest dog shows in the 1860s.",
  "Low-slung soldiers' dogs": "The short-legged dogs said to have been left in the Wicklow glens by Flemish and Hessian mercenaries given land by Elizabeth I. Long-bodied and heavy, they were crossed with local Irish terriers to make the Glen of Imaal. The origin is tradition, not record.",
  "Ancient Celtic earth dogs": "The pre-Roman low hunting dogs of the Celtic peoples across northern Europe, built to pursue quarry into its burrow, and a deep root of Britain's earth-working terriers. Most short-legged breeds share one genetic change: an extra copy of the FGF4 gene, which shortens the limbs.",
  "Early Badger hunting dogs": "The long, low earth dogs the Celts are said to have brought to Cardiganshire, short-legged and hard enough to face a badger in its sett. They feed the corgi cattle-dog line rather than the terriers. An adult badger outweighs most terriers and bites through bone.",
  "Old short-legged working dogs": "A broad low-bodied utility group, kept for jobs that suited a dog close to the ground: bolting vermin, working around farms, and turning the roasting spit. The turnspit ran inside a wheel by the hearth, driving the spit by treadmill, ordinary Tudor kitchen kit.",
  "Farm and kitchen curs": "Farm and kitchen curs were the humble household dogs of old Britain, rough-built and unpedigreed, set to whatever needed doing: killing rats, guarding the yard, turning the spit. 'Cur' was never a breed but a catch-all for the ordinary working mongrel, and in this reconstruction they feed the Turnspit and drover-cur lines. The word carried real contempt, a byword for a worthless dog, which is partly why no one recorded what these useful animals actually were.",

  // Batch 3 (19 August 2026): spaniels and water dogs. Keys verified against
  // data/lineage.ts by exact name. Includes the continental water dogs (Barbet
  // water dogs, Barbet-type water dogs, Old European water dogs, Continental
  // water dogs): they are integral to the British and Irish water-spaniel and
  // retriever story in a way the continental ratters were not, and Continental
  // water dogs is 45% of the Irish Water Spaniel. Barbet-type water dogs had no
  // breedInfo entry. Continental water dogs keeps its note's hedge: the record is
  // not precise enough to name which continental breed shaped the Irish Water
  // Spaniel, and the write-up does not resolve it. Deferred to the Toys batch:
  // Old toy spaniels, Old sporting toy spaniels, Continental toy spaniels.
  "Land Spaniels": "The broad early flushing-spaniel stock of Britain, worked on land to find and flush game for the hawk, the net and later the gun, and the shared root of the whole spaniel family. Spaniel comes from the Old French espaigneul, the Spanish dog.",
  "Old Welsh Land Spaniels": "The hardy red-and-white flushing dogs of Wales, worked in rough hill country to spring game for the net and gun, and the direct root of the Welsh Springer. That breed still carries only red and white, a colour fixed long before Kennel Club recognition.",
  "Heavier working Spaniels": "The broad, low, substantial end of the old land-spaniel stock, built for pushing through thick cover rather than ranging fast, and the source of the Sussex and Clumber's bone. The Sussex gives tongue as it works, so a slow dog could be followed by ear.",
  "Old setting spaniels": "The old setting spaniels were the early bird-finding dogs that crept up on game and then crouched, or 'set', low to the ground so a net could be drawn over both dog and covey. In this reconstruction they are the root of the whole setter family. The crouch was the point: before shooting flying birds became normal, the fowler netted them, and a dog that froze rather than flushed was worth far more than one that ran in.",
  "Water Spaniels": "The old rough-coated working water dogs of Britain's rivers, fens and marshes, curly or wavy in coat and bred to retrieve waterfowl from cold water. Caius, in 1576, listed the water spaniel or finder as a distinct kind, so the type was already recognised.",
  "Old Irish water dogs": "The rougher, less standardised liver-coated water retrievers of Ireland, worked to fetch duck and other fowl from bog, lough and river, and the stock the Irish Water Spaniel came from. That breed kept their oddest feature, a smooth rat tail bare of curls.",
  "English Water Spaniel": "Britain's own curly or liver-and-white water retriever, worked on the fens to fetch duck before the modern retrievers took the job. Never numerous, it faded as the Labrador rose and was effectively extinct by the 1930s, though its blood carried into the Curly-Coated Retriever.",
  "Rough water dogs": "The shaggy, practical water-working dogs of northern Europe and Britain, their thick oily coats a defence against cold water rather than a show feature. The coat did what a wetsuit does: a dense outer layer shed water while a woolly undercoat trapped warmth.",
  "Fishermen's water dogs": "The practical coastal and river dogs of working people, kept to retrieve fish and gear that slipped the net, haul lines, guard the boat and swim a rope ashore. A dog that would dive for a dropped fish saved a fisherman real money.",
  "Barbet water dogs": "The rustic French marsh retrievers, shaggy and woolly-coated, broad-headed and bred to fetch waterfowl from river and fen rather than clipped for show. The name comes from the French barbe, beard, after the thick facial hair, and they are among the Poodle's older roots.",
  "Barbet-type water dogs": "The Barbet-type water dogs were the broader family of curly water dogs of the European mainland, kin to the Barbet but not one fixed breed. Buoyant and shaggy, they worked marsh and river across the continent, and in this reconstruction they belong to the water-dog stock behind the British and Irish water spaniels. The same curly, water-shedding coat turns up right across Europe, in the Poodle, the Portuguese and Spanish water dogs and the Irish Water Spaniel.",
  "Old European water dogs": "The broad shaggy, curly or woolly water-working stock of the continent, the deep root behind the Barbet, the Poodle, the Iberian water dogs and Britain's water spaniels. The Poodle began among them as a duck retriever: its German name Pudel comes from pudeln, to splash.",
  "Continental water dogs": "The continental water dogs were the curly-coated water dogs of the European mainland, from clipped, refined retrievers to rustic bearded French marsh dogs. In this reconstruction they are the largest single strand behind the Irish Water Spaniel. But which of them shaped it is unknown: the record is not precise enough to name the breed, and this tree leaves the question open rather than invent an answer for the tallest and one of the oldest spaniels.",
};
